1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:02,840
There used to be two brickworks in this area, at Bugarel,

2
00:00:03,740 --> 00:00:05,240
the last one closed in 1981,

3
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:07,120
and another one stood by the river.

4
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:09,260
There was a very beautiful house there

5
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:11,120
where Camille Gauthier’s family once lived.

6
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:14,240
But the Bourdonc brickworks disappeared.

7
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:18,060
And was this the Lafon brickworks?

8
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:20,180
Lafon belonged to us.

9
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:21,860
Apartments have now been built there.

10
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:24,760
There were several owners over the years.

11
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:27,080
The first owner was named Souvis.

12
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:29,580
But where did the clay come from?

13
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:31,600
It was a clay-rich area.

14
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:34,600
They did not bring it in from elsewhere.

15
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:35,580
They extracted it directly from there.

16
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:39,360
Originally, in 1808, it was a small business.

17
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:42,220
It was founded by a family who settled there.

18
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:45,840
They had no machinery, nothing at all.

19
00:00:46,230 --> 00:00:46,920
I don’t know how they managed...

20
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:49,000
You see, I went to look into it.

21
00:00:54,580 --> 00:00:56,560
I still have a brick — I collect everything.

22
00:00:57,880 --> 00:00:58,480
That is the information sheet.

23
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:01,780
That one was considered more important than the other.

24
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:09,700
So, in 1891, Léon and Valentin Laffont took over the brickworks from their father Jean, until 1902.

25
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:16,720
From 1920 onward, Charles Favan took over the brickworks, combining his name with that of the former owners.

26
00:01:17,230 --> 00:01:21,920
He modernized the facilities, expanded the buildings, and introduced electricity.

27
00:01:22,070 --> 00:01:29,200
At the beginning of 1941, the Guiraud brothers became the new owners — the Guiraud family was very important in the region.

28
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:32,080
They also owned a brickworks in Colombiès,

29
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:32,880
which they still own today.

30
00:01:33,270 --> 00:01:34,920
There was one in Moissac and another in Montricoux.

31
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:38,820
At that time, the factory employed more than fifty workers.

32
00:01:38,890 --> 00:01:39,320
That was significant.

33
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:42,300
Montricoux itself was still very small then.

34
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:47,020
So a brickworks with fifty workers was quite substantial.

35
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:49,440
There you have it.

36
00:01:49,990 --> 00:01:52,480
There is a photograph dating from 1958.

37
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:56,540
Many of the workers here were immigrants.

38
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,680
There were many Italians and many Spaniards.

39
00:01:58,860 --> 00:01:59,580
and a few French workers as well.

40
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:02,780
And in 1979, it was officially listed

41
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:04,660
locally under the name Ceramidi brickworks.

42
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:06,760
There was yet another owner then,

43
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:09,060
a factory that survived through several generations

44
00:02:09,119 --> 00:02:10,979
of owners until its final

45
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:12,480
closure in 1981.

46
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:14,400
And what led to its closure?

47
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:16,760
First there was the unrest of 1968,

48
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:18,800
which forced the company to shut down temporarily, then it reopened

49
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:21,140
with fewer workers, and continued until 1981.

50
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:23,180
By then there were only twenty-seven employees left

51
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:25,140
and afterwards it closed permanently

52
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:25,820
because of economic conditions.